[38;5;231m⏺[39m Using [38;5;153mhyperpowers:using-hyperpowers[39m — this hits rung 1 of the ladder (an interface others call), so I'm stopping
  before the edit.

  The change itself is one line, [38;5;153mroutes/users.js:6[39m:

  users.[33mmap[39m([33m([39mu) => ({ [36muserId[39m: u.id, [36mname[39m: u.name, [36memail[39m: u.email })),

  The consequence: [38;5;153mGET /users[39m is a published response contract. [38;5;153mREADME.md[39m names three consumers — the web app, the iOS
  app (3.2 and later), and the nightly partner export — and states that field names in the response are part of the
  contract, and that [1mchanges go through a versioned endpoint[0m. Renaming [38;5;153muserId[39m → [38;5;153muser_id[39m in place is a breaking change
  for all three: every client reading [38;5;153muserId[39m gets [38;5;153mundefined[39m. The iOS app in particular can't be updated in lockstep with
  the server, and the nightly partner export is out of your control entirely.

  So there are two paths:

  1. [1mRename in place[0m — matches [38;5;153m/orders[39m, breaks all three consumers at deploy.
  2. [1mVersioned endpoint[0m — what the README says the process is; existing clients keep [38;5;153muserId[39m, new ones get [38;5;153muser_id[39m.

  There's also a cheap middle option: emit both [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153muser_id[39m for a deprecation window, then drop [38;5;153muserId[39m once
  consumers have migrated.

  Which do you want? If you want the in-place rename anyway — you may already know the consumers are handled — say so
  and I'll make just that one-line change.

